ani and Michael I. Jordan},\n199: booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n202: title = {Active Learning with Statistical Models},\n204: year = {1996}\n208:@article{Dempster,\n209: title = {Maximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm},\n210: author = {A. P. Dempster and Nan M. Laird and Donald B. Rubin},\n211: year = {1977},\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-07T06:45:47.420116+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"ARXIV:2210.07316\",\n    \"ARXIV:2009.03300\",\n    \"ARXIV:2211.09110\",\n    \"ARXIV:2402.14992\",\n    \"ARXIV:2407.12844\",\n    \"ARXIV:2407.13696\",\n    \"ARXIV:2405.10938\",\n    \"ARXIV:2412.06540\",\n    \"DOI:10.1109/MC.2009.263\",\n    \"DOI:10.1109/ICDM.2008.22\",\n    \"DOI:10.1007/s10208-009-9045-5\",\n    \"DOI:10.1109/TIT.2010.2044061\",\n    \"DOI:10.1109/TIT.2010.2046205\",\n    \"TITLE:Spectral Regularization Algorithms for Learning Large Incomplete Matrices\",\n    \"TITLE:Probabilistic Matrix Factorization\",\n    \"ARXIV:1212.0467\",\n    \"DOI:10.1145/1390156.1390267\",\n    \"DOI:10.1214/aos/1176344552\",\n    \"DOI:10.1093/biomet/63.3.581\",\n    \"DOI:10.1037/1082-989X.7.2.147\",\n    \"DOI:10.1214/12-AOAS547\",\n    \"DOI:10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634\",\n    \"DOI:10.1214/ss/1177009939\",\n    \"DOI:10.1613/jair.295\",\n    \"DOI:10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01600.x\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.allowed.MTEB\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"ARXIV:2210.07316\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"MTEB\",\n      \"title\": \"MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark\",\n      \"year\": 2022,\n      \"query\": \"ARXIV:2210.07316\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/ARXIV:2210.07316\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-07T06:45:47.420116+00:00\",\n      \"content_path\": \"literature/MTEB.txt\",\n      \"read_scope\": \"abstract\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.allowed.MMLU\",\n{\n  \"ablation_studies\": [],\n  \"all_programs\": [],\n  \"best_any\": null,\n  \"best_correct\": null,\n  \"downstream_study\": {\n    \"attempt_counts\": {\n      \"scored\": 0,\n      \"total\": 3,\n      \"unscored\": 3\n    },\n    \"baseline_program_dir\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261107_045228/investigation/.baseline-seed-6ewnjfck/program_0\",\n    \"baseline_score\": 0.9527030000575736,\n    \"baseline_tree_sha256\": \"33fc15401729399a8a88a6f041ce38f1279d47925bd6d123e805fb82ff752560\",\n    \"combined_score\": 0.9527030000575736,\n    \"entrypoint_path\": \"src/program.py\",\n    \"final_evaluation\": {\n      \"correct\": true,\n      \"outcome\": \"qualified\",\n      \"status\": \"completed\"\n    },\n    \"investigation_outcome\": \"completed_no_winner\",\n    \"limitations\": [\n      \"Investigation found no qualifying winner; selection used search evidence only.\",\n      \"Unscored attempts are missing performance evidence, not measured losses.\",\n      \"Search scores and sealed final metrics belong to different evaluation scopes.\",\n      \"An unqualified final outcome may reflect validity or other criteria; do not infer that failure means only an inferior score. Interpret metrics within those limitations.\"\n    ],\n    \"program_dir\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261107_045228/downstream/program\",\n    \"program_tree_sha256\": \"33fc15401729399a8a88a6f041ce38f1279d47925bd6d123e805fb82ff752560\",\n    \"search_score_delta\": 0.0,\n    \"selection_kind\": \"baseline\",\n    \"source_attempt_id\": \"bootstrap\",\n    \"source_evidence\": {\n      \"baseline\": \"source_evidence/downstream/baseline\",\n      \"program\": \"source_evidence/downstream/program\"\n    },\n    \"source_program_dir\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261107_045228/investigation/.baseline-seed-6ewnjfck/program_0\",\n    \"source_tree_sha256\": \"33fc15401729399a8a88a6f041ce38f1279d47925bd6d123e805fb82ff752560\"\n  },\n7:  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the sealed final comparison of one retained method against five operational baselines across two sources, two split regimes, and three seeds, with a paired uncertainty interval against random-query low-rank prediction and a dependence-destroying control. Present MTEB embedding tasks and Open LLM generative-model benchmarks as separate evidence blocks under a shared score-matrix question; fit each source independently, without cross-source model alignment or transfer claims. The contribution is a transparent empirical assessment and reproducible mechanism description, not a newly discovered winning architecture. The alternatives have no measured performance evidence. The informative limitations, especially method-dependent remaining targets and non-response-adaptive selection, belong in the central argument rather than only in the supplement.\",\n12:    \"The staged selected and original-baseline source files are byte-identical. Study selection is the original baseline, not an improved search winner; do not manufacture a second final comparator for the same source.\",\n13:    \"The final evidence supplies the actual scientific comparison despite the absence of scored search alternatives. The final qualification record is read independently of the downstream selection summary.\",\n15:    \"The current bibliography/provenance contains retrieved factorization-related metadata but lacks the permitted starter and dataset records needed for domain positioning. This limits literature claims, not the coherence of the measured comparison; the writer must verify relevant allowed records before citing them and must not fill pages with unrelated retrieval hits. No network retrieval is performed in this framing stage.\"\n26:      \"include_reason\": \"The closest selector comparison: source inspection confirms the same factor-fitting and prediction construction with random available queries. A pooled paired family-clustered interval is available against this comparator; different remaining targets still prevent a fixed-target causal interpretation.\"\n31:      \"include_reason\": \"Strongest pooled nonfactor comparator. It changes representation, regularization selection, and potentially query sets, so it is a joint pipeline comparison rather than a matched-query reconstruction ablation. Retain the Open LLM family-split seed where it slightly exceeds the studied method.\"\n36:      \"include_reason\": \"Tests a simpler correlation-centrality query ordering with covariance reconstruction; provides an interpretable selection comparator without adding an unevaluated method family.\"\n41:      \"include_reason\": \"Observation-invariant reference for the substantial predictability already present in historical task means. Its operational score is computed on its own query-policy remainder, not a universal zero-query target set.\"\n46:      \"include_reason\": \"Simple purchased-score reference: shift historical benchmark means by the observed mean residual. Distinguishes structured reconstruction from a global model-level offset.\"\n51:    \"Pooled evidence: studied method 0.9527636891226994, random-query low-rank 0.9364101694132948, information-selected ridge 0.9482262260487015, medoid ridge 0.9327472829724573, mean-offset 0.920605668187274, and historical means 0.90687688776656. Use a compact table, not a redundant figure. Only the studied-versus-random-low-rank comparison has a supplied paired interval: [0.014807167765235746, 0.01847801943997085]. Do not attach that interval to another comparator or a source/split cell.\",\n52:    \"Source evidence remains distinct: MTEB pooled score 0.9468974985138557 and Open LLM pooled score 0.9586298797315429 describe different task and budget regimes, not a controlled difficulty comparison. MTEB is embedding-model evidence, not evidence about generative LLM benchmark suites.\",\n53:    \"Dependence control: compare actual and independently column-deranged historical training scores for each source/split with paired seeds; pooled deranged score 0.9233917542059907 and actual-minus-deranged score 0.029371934916708625. The control supports dependence on historical alignment, not unique causal attribution to the acquisition rule or immunity to leakage.\",\n56:    \"Private-only provenance: study.selection_kind=baseline; study.investigation_outcome=completed_no_winner; study.search_score_delta=0.0; study.attempts.total=3, scored=0, unscored=3. These establish no measured search gain and no comparative losses for alternative methods. Search and final scores must not be subtracted as improvement.\",\n61:    \"Figure 2, main comparison: four horizontal dot-plot panels, arranged by source and split, compare all six evaluated methods using actual/nonreplay case scores; display three seed points and a clearly distinguished mean per method.\",\n62:    \"Figure 3, dependence control: two source panels compare actual versus column-deranged historical training in each split using paired seed observations; use no training-time or search-trajectory axis.\"\n68:      \"purpose\": \"Make the score-matrix object, query/prediction mechanism, and boundaries of the evidence legible before the numerical comparisons.\",\n86:      \"figure_id\": \"main_comparison\",\n88:      \"purpose\": \"Show the direct six-method comparison and its source/split/seed heterogeneity, including the retained local reversal.\",\n127:    \"purpose\": \"After the references, provide reproducible implementation and protocol depth plus the complete retained numerical evidence, without moving central caveats, the main baseline contrast, or the dependence control out of the body.\",\n156:        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented role of LLM agents in alternative-method ideation/implementation and this framing/writing workflow. Distinguish unscored generated alternatives from evaluated retained code; do not assert that an LLM trained the evaluated models, ran unrecorded experiments, or independently verified literature. The writing-stage author must add its actual role without inventing undocumented human supervision.\",\n194:    \"Main-body structure and inclusive page targets: title/abstract 0.45 page; Introduction with early overview 1.05; Related Work 0.65; Problem and Scope 0.65; Masked Low-Rank Selection and Reconstruction 1.65; Evaluation Design 1.0; Prediction and Historical-Dependence Results 1.8; Discussion and Limitations 0.95; Conclusion 0.30. Total target 8.5 body pages, with figures/tables included in those allocations, not added afterward. The hard venue range is 8-9 pages excluding references; an 8-page body needs its last page at least 75% full. Use extra space for equations, baseline definitions, aggregation and target-policy explanation, not unmeasured experiments or broad literature filler.\",\n198:    \"No new ablations, fixed-target evaluations, family-validation results, nonlinear wins, rank-sensitivity sweeps, calibration curves, real benchmark execution savings, GPU comparisons, or universal k thresholds may be planned as completed evidence. Budget tables answer only what the retained checkpoint scores show; no error-tolerance target was prespecified.\",\n199:    \"The supplied paired interval supports only the pooled comparison with random-query low-rank prediction under the observed objective. For all other comparisons use observed-score language and display seed variability without inventing p-values or confidence intervals. Do not count replay as a fourth seed or assume three seeds are independent model families.\",\n201:    \"Keep all central comparisons, the actual-versus-deranged control, and essential caveats in the main text. Route full case/checkpoint tables, expanded equations, acquisition/environment details and documented unmeasured alternatives after references under Supplementary Information. Any later authoritative appendix ranking may add material there without changing this strategy.\",\n203:    \"Literature guardrail: the current retrieved metadata is not a read/verified domain literature review. Cite only relevant keys actually present in references.bib after checking their provenance and permitted content. Missing starter/data records require narrowly scoped exact-title or exact-identifier resolution and actual reading before citation; never search for the forbidden prior study, traverse citation graphs, guess its identity, or invent missing entries. Log and stop reading any incidental forbidden material. Do not import the retrieval list's unrelated application papers merely to satisfy page count.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
