{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,12p' claims_grounding.json; sed -n '13,25p' claims_grounding.json; sed -n '180,220p' paper.tex; tail -12 memory.md; cat log_paper_event.py; sed -n '1,120p' literature/Metabench.txt; sed -n '1,100p' literature/BenchBench.txt\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"scope\": \"Final evaluation results are independent of search selection. Implementation descriptions use the inspectable retained source, without claiming byte-identical final-snapshot identity.\",\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"The final evaluation completed and qualified; qualification is not proof of broad generalization.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The retained original reference has zero measured search score change; three alternative evaluations are unscored and do not establish measured losses or failure causes.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.investigation_outcome\", \"study.search_score_delta\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.unscored\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The pooled studied score is 0.9527636891 and equivalent normalized MAE is 0.0472363109; rounded values in the abstract, results, and pooled table are 0.9528 and 0.0472.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Random-query low-rank pooled score is 0.9364101694, equivalent MAE 0.0635898306; the studied-minus-random difference is 0.0163535197.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.random_lowrank_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The supplied pooled paired family-clustered interval against random-query factors is [0.0148071678, 0.0184780194], not an interval against ridge or for subgroups.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_low\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_high\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Information-selected ridge scores 0.9482262260 (MAE 0.0517737740), with descriptive studied-minus-ridge difference 0.0045374631 and no supplied paired significance interval.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.selected_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Medoid ridge scores 0.9327472830 (MAE 0.0672527170), mean offset 0.9206056682 (MAE 0.0793943318), and historical means 0.9068768878 (MAE 0.0931231122).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.medoid_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.copy_score\", \"final.metric.constant_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The studied method has the highest pooled observed score among the six evaluated policies, without statistical superiority being established against all comparators.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.random_lowrank_score\", \"final.metric.selected_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.medoid_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.copy_score\", \"final.metric.constant_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Three final seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019; replay is excluded from primary means and figures.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#aggregation\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Figure 2, supplementary Table 2, and derived_results.json retain each of the twelve primary source/split/seed cells and six policies, using control=false and replay=false.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"build_paper_artifacts.py\", \"derived_results.json\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The studied policy has higher observed scores than random-query factors in all twelve retained primary cells and the highest three-seed mean in each of four source/split conditions.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"derived_results.json#conditions\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Studied condition means are 0.9496/0.9442 for MTEB model/family splits and 0.9591/0.9582 for Open LLM model/family splits, rounded from saved observations.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"derived_results.json#conditions\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pooled source scores are 0.9468974985 for MTEB and 0.9586298797 for Open LLM; they do not constitute controlled source-difficulty comparisons.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.mteb_score\", \"final.metric.open_llm_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Open LLM family split at seed 1019 gives studied score 0.9531041725 versus information-selected ridge 0.9533160600, a retained local reversal.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes[2].solution.cases\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pooled independently deranged-history score is 0.9233917542 and actual-minus-control gap is 0.0293719349.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.permuted_score\", \"final.metric.corruption_gap\", \"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Every retained source/split/seed pair has a lower observed studied-policy score under historical-column derangement, as shown in Figure 3 and supplementary Table 3; the contrast changes learning, querying, and target composition jointly.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.corruption_gap\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#permute_training\", \"tables/controls.tex\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The final required cases completed and reset replay passed; these are interface/execution controls, not comprehensive validity guarantees.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.failed_episode_fraction\", \"final.metric.control_failure_fraction\", \"final.metric.reset_replay_ok\", \"final.qualification.status\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Supplementary Figure 4 shows only the studied-policy measured checkpoint arrays, increasing in each retained actual trajectory; no baseline checkpoint comparison, fixed-panel curve, or universally sufficient budget is inferred.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Recorded seed durations are 24.0816306723, 22.8890082091, 23.0760153015 seconds, excluding independent acquisition; no measured inference-cost, end-to-end storage, or speed advantage is asserted.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The inspectable retained source implements masked alternating ridge factors, fixed observed column means, and SVD initialization; lambda=0.1, candidate ranks 1/2/4/8, seeded 10% cell validation, at most four validation sweeps and eight refit sweeps capped by requested steps.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl\", \"Koren\", \"Jain\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L45-L83\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Equations 1 and 3 describe clipped remaining-target 1-MAE and context-independent ridge latent prediction; availability is known and historical missing cells are masked, not observed zeros.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#curve_score\", \"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L110-L142\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The retained-source acquisition in Equation 4 uses a unit-noise quadratic trace-reduction proxy, includes the queried coordinate, and ignores purchased score values. Equation 5 is a fixed-target matrix identity, not an MAE optimality theorem.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Chaloner\", \"Cohn\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L114-L127\"]},\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permuted_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/historical_dependence_control.pdf}\n\\caption{Historical-correspondence control by source. Each line joins aligned and independently column-deranged histories for one retained seed and split; it does not depict temporal change. Color and marker distinguish split definitions. The control changes the learned representation and potentially the query set, so the score gap is not an acquisition-only effect.}\n\\label{fig:control}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThis is a dependence check, not proof that the low-rank model is true. The control perturbs factor learning, selection, and prediction jointly, and could also alter which targets remain. It cannot identify the best latent rank, verify that missingness is ignorable, or certify absence of benchmark contamination. All required final cases completed, and the reset replay passed; these facts support execution consistency within the tested interface, not comprehensive scientific validity.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reset_replay_ok]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the comparison identifies.}\nThe evidence is strongest for a policy-level statement: for these public score matrices, splits, and checkpoint weights, variance-guided factor reconstruction improves the observed remaining-target objective over its random-query counterpart. This is a useful decision problem when the evaluator cares precisely about whichever available benchmarks were not purchased. It is a weaker basis for a statement about reconstructing a prespecified panel, because the policy controls the scored target composition as well as the information collected. A fixed untouched panel, shared query sets for predictor ablations, and a selector-only comparison on that panel would separate these effects; none is reported here.\n\n\\paragraph{What the mechanism does not establish.}\nThe acquisition formula uses a conditional quadratic proxy with a unit-noise working interpretation. No empirical calibration of that uncertainty is available. The optimized proxy includes a coordinate that subsequently leaves the target set, and the final score uses absolute rather than squared loss. These mismatches do not invalidate the observed contrast, but they prevent interpreting it as validation of an optimality theorem. Likewise, availability-conditioned sequential choices should not be described as learning which question to ask from a new model's answers: the implemented choice ignores those answers.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope of the data distribution.}\nHistorical scores are selectively observed. Coverage filtering and the supplied availability mask restrict the population to models with enough measured benchmarks, potentially excluding the hardest prospective cases. Name-derived family labels can miss cross-namespace relationships and do not guarantee separation of pretraining data, fine-tuning lineages, or shared evaluation contamination. Historical-cell rank selection also does not measure sensitivity to tuning on entirely unseen families. The final splits and derangement control constrain some interpretations, but they do not justify arbitrary out-of-distribution or future-model generalization. Implementation interpretation uses the inspectable retained source; an independently verifiable byte-identical final snapshot is unavailable, as detailed in the supplement.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty, costs, and practical use.}\nThe supplied bootstrap interval concerns one pooled matched-factor contrast under heuristic family clustering. It is not evidence of significance against all baselines; subgroup differences and the ridge comparison remain descriptive. The experiment purchases benchmark scores retrospectively from a frozen table and assumes equal query costs. It therefore demonstrates prediction under a score budget, not measured savings in inference time, energy, or monetary cost. A practical evaluator should select its own error tolerance, retain direct measurement of safety-critical or poorly represented benchmarks, and test a fixed target panel before relying on inferred scores for model release decisions.\n\n\\paragraph{What averaging hides.}\nEqual row weights give sparse and dense held-out models the same contribution after their target errors are averaged. Equal source weights likewise prevent the larger generative-model table from overwhelming the embedding-model setting. These are explicit priorities of the evaluation, not invariances of the underlying inference problem. A different deployment population, benchmark weighting, or cost model can change the policy ranking. Moreover, a small average absolute error can coexist with large errors on a particular capability. The retained summaries contain neither per-benchmark residual distributions nor tail-error intervals, so they do not support certifying individual benchmark predictions, identifying which tasks can safely be skipped, or converting the pooled score into a deployment-specific reliability threshold. Reporting the source/split views and the local ridge reversal is therefore necessary, but not a substitute for task-level validation when choosing an actual evaluation panel.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nHistorical leaderboard measurements can support budgeted reconstruction without fitting a large predictor or running new language-model inference. In the evaluated setting, a regularized masked factor model coupled to variance-guided queries yields a higher pooled remaining-target score than the same factor construction with random queries. The historical-column control further shows that useful correspondence across benchmarks contributes to the measured result. These observations hold across the separately fitted embedding-model and generative-model sources and their two split definitions, rather than establishing transfer of a common representation.\n\nThe most important qualification is the target itself. Query policies remove different coordinates from evaluation, and the strongest covariance comparator is competitive, including a retained local reversal. The measurements therefore do not establish fixed-target superiority, score-adaptive selection, calibrated uncertainty, or a universal number of sufficient benchmarks. The appropriate conclusion is narrower: variance-guided masked reconstruction is a useful operational policy in the observed regime, with clear reasons to require shared-target evaluation, stronger family definitions, and explicit deployment costs before making broader claims.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n- evidence: Final completed/qualified; static plots exclude replay. Retained source and live downstream entrypoint match; sealed and selection tree digests differ, so exact final snapshot identity explicitly not certified. Rejected mismatched bibliographic record and contaminated abstract; replaced through exact title/DOI verification. No forbidden literature encountered and no experiments rerun.\n- next_action: Map all claims, compile, inspect page geometry and figures, repair validation failures.\n\n### 2026-11-07T06:57:41.000891+00:00 — verify\n- summary: Grounding and citation checks pass after localizing evidence; official ICLR 2026 style verified.\n- evidence: 37 claims, 25 consulted references, zero dangling evidence after sidecar repair. Original source audit/plan/evaluator copied unchanged into source_evidence/evaluation because verifier rejects symlink-escaped targets. Supplied style differs from official only by venue header; using official review style with unchanged geometry. Canonical compile input checker rejects a legitimate cross-root TeX distribution symlink; manual compilation and independent canonical-realpath audit planned.\n- next_action: Check final body occupancy, rendered SI, figure density, source code claims and language; finalize reproducibility report.\n\n### 2026-11-07T07:04:16.004223+00:00 — final\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs; local manuscript checks have no hard errors.\n- evidence: Final evidence read independently: completed/qualified; no measured improvement beyond original reference, three unscored alternatives retained as missing evidence. 37 claim mappings, 25 consulted references, eight-page body with approximately 92.6% final-page geometric extent; zero dangling scientific evidence. Retained local ridge reversal, different remaining targets, non-response-adaptive selection, heuristic family/missingness/tuning limits, and unresolved exact sealed-source snapshot identity. Canonical figure validator lacks src.orchestrator; canonical compile-recorder rejects a legitimate TeX cross-root system symlink. Sanitized compilation, canonical citation/claim/SI checks, schema/density checks, numerical consistency and independent resolved-dependency audit pass. No new experiments run; conclusions gate=keep.\n- next_action: Parent should render the orchestrator-owned conceptual overview and perform final canonical validation/recompile; do not overwrite frozen final metrics or the concept-complete marker.\nimport datetime\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nevent = {'timestamp': datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n         'scope': 'paper', 'step': sys.argv[1], 'summary': sys.argv[2],\n         'evidence': sys.argv[3], 'next_action': sys.argv[4]}\nmarkdown = '\\n### ' + event['timestamp'] + ' — ' + event['step'] + '\\n'\nmarkdown += '\\n'.join('- ' + key + ': ' + event[key] for key in ['summary', 'evidence', 'next_action']) + '\\n'\nfor path in [Path('memory.md'), Path('global_memory').resolve() / 'memory.md']:\n    with path.open('a') as handle:\n        handle.write(markdown)\nwith (Path('global_memory').resolve() / 'memory.jsonl').open('a') as handle:\n    handle.write(json.dumps(event, ensure_ascii=False) + '\\n')\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) vary in their abilities on a range of tasks. Initiatives such as the Open LLM Leaderboard aim to quantify these differences with several large benchmarks (sets of test items to which an LLM can respond either correctly or incorrectly). However, high correlations within and between benchmark scores suggest that (1) there exists a small set of common underlying abilities that these benchmarks measure, and (2) items tap into redundant information and the benchmarks may thus be considerably compressed. We use data from n>5000 LLMs to identify the most informative items of six benchmarks, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag, MMLU, TruthfulQA and WinoGrande (with d = 28,632 items in total). From them we distill a sparse benchmark, metabench, that has less than 3% of the original size of all six benchmarks combined. This new sparse benchmark goes beyond point scores by yielding estimators of the underlying benchmark-specific abilities. We show that these estimators (1) can be used to reconstruct each original individual benchmark score with, on average, 1.24% root mean square error (RMSE), (2) reconstruct the original total score with 0.58% RMSE, and (3) have a single underlying common factor whose Spearman correlation with the total score is r = 0.94.Recent advancements in Language Models (LMs) have catalyzed the creation of multiple benchmarks, designed to assess these models' general capabilities. A crucial task, however, is assessing the validity of the benchmarks themselves. This is most commonly done via Benchmark Agreement Testing (BAT), where new benchmarks are validated against established ones using some agreement metric (e.g., rank correlation). Despite the crucial role of BAT for benchmark builders and consumers, there are no standardized procedures for such agreement testing. This deficiency can lead to invalid conclusions, fostering mistrust in benchmarks and upending the ability to properly choose the appropriate benchmark to use. By analyzing over 40 prominent benchmarks, we demonstrate how some overlooked methodological choices can significantly influence BAT results, potentially undermining the validity of conclusions. To address these inconsistencies, we propose a set of best practices for BAT and demonstrate how utilizing these methodologies greatly improves BAT robustness and validity. To foster adoption and facilitate future research,, we introduce BenchBench, a python package for BAT, and release the BenchBench-leaderboard, a meta-benchmark designed to evaluate benchmarks using their peers. Our findings underscore the necessity for standardized BAT, ensuring the robustness and validity of benchmark evaluations in the evolving landscape of language model research. BenchBench Package: github.com/IBM/BenchBench Leaderboard: hf.co/spaces/IBM/BenchBench","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
