{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py; sed -n '22,30p' claims_grounding.json; sed -n '182,202p' paper.tex; sed -n '107,113p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,5p' literature/Cohn.txt; sed -n '1,5p' literature/Chaloner.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py; sed -n '22,30p' claims_grounding.json; sed -n '182,202p' paper.tex; sed -n '107,113p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,5p' literature/Cohn.txt; sed -n '1,5p' literature/Chaloner.txt\"","aggregated_output":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\n\ndef _markdown_entry(event: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Handle markdown entry logic for this module.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"### {event['timestamp']} | step={event['step']} | scope={event['scope']}\",\n        f\"- summary: {event['summary']}\",\n        f\"- evidence: {event['evidence']}\",\n        f\"- next_action: {event['next_action']}\",\n    ]\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = event.get(key)\n        if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip():\n            lines.append(f\"- {key}: {value.strip()}\")\n\n    metrics = event.get(\"metrics\")\n    if isinstance(metrics, dict) and metrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- metrics: {json.dumps(metrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    rubrics = event.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if isinstance(rubrics, list) and rubrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- rubrics: {json.dumps(rubrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    tags = event.get(\"tags\")\n    if isinstance(tags, list) and tags:\n        lines.append(f\"- tags: {', '.join(str(item) for item in tags)}\")\n\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef _append_text(path: Path, text: str, *, header: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Safely initialize and append text to a regular file.\"\"\"\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if not size:\n            _write_all(fd, header.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n        elif os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _append_jsonl(path: Path, payload: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Append one JSON object as a JSONL record.\"\"\"\n    line = (json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"utf-8\")\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if size and os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, line)\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _open_regular_for_append(path: Path) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Open an append target without following its parent or leaf.\"\"\"\n    directory_fd = _walk_directory_no_follow(path.parent)\n    try:\n        fd = os.open(\n            path.name,\n            os.O_APPEND\n            | os.O_CREAT\n            | os.O_RDWR\n            | getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)\n            | getattr(os, \"O_NONBLOCK\", 0),\n            0o644,\n            dir_fd=directory_fd,\n        )\n    finally:\n        os.close(directory_fd)\n    if stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):\n        return fd\n    os.close(fd)\n    raise OSError(f\"unsafe non-regular append target: {path}\")\n\n\n    {\"claim\": \"Recorded seed durations are 24.0816306723, 22.8890082091, 23.0760153015 seconds, excluding independent acquisition; no measured inference-cost, end-to-end storage, or speed advantage is asserted.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The inspectable retained source implements masked alternating ridge factors, fixed observed column means, and SVD initialization; lambda=0.1, candidate ranks 1/2/4/8, seeded 10% cell validation, at most four validation sweeps and eight refit sweeps capped by requested steps.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl\", \"Koren\", \"Jain\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L45-L83\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Equations 1 and 3 describe clipped remaining-target 1-MAE and context-independent ridge latent prediction; availability is known and historical missing cells are masked, not observed zeros.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#curve_score\", \"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L110-L142\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The retained-source acquisition in Equation 4 uses a unit-noise quadratic trace-reduction proxy, includes the queried coordinate, and ignores purchased score values. Equation 5 is a fixed-target matrix identity, not an MAE optimality theorem.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Chaloner\", \"Cohn\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L114-L127\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Random LF uses matching reference factor fit/prediction construction; the ridge comparators use zero-filled centered covariance and penalties 0.01/0.1/1 chosen with every-fifth-row historical validation; means and mean-offset policies use random legal queries.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#reference_fit,predict_factors,run_baseline\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The readable retained and original reference files match, but exact byte-level identity to the separately hashed final snapshot is not independently certified; mechanism interpretation is conditional on supplied-source association.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\", \"source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The evaluated source regimes are separately fitted MTEB embedding-model scores and Open LLM six raw generative benchmark columns; no learned cross-source transfer is asserted.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MTEB\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#sources,extraction\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#build_case\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pinned MTEB revision is 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383; pinned Open LLM revision is 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2. MTEB extraction uses natural-bound original test scores and default/English task/revision/subset columns; Open LLM uses raw scores. The latter dataset license is undeclared.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MTEB\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#sources,extraction\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The inspected final protocol uses model and heuristic name-family disjoint groups, approximately 70% historical groups, twenty-observation MTEB column support, and coverage filters; family labels are not authoritative lineage.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#split_components,require_disjoint_rows,build_case\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#extraction\"]},\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/historical_dependence_control.pdf}\n\\caption{Historical-correspondence control by source. Each line joins aligned and independently column-deranged histories for one retained seed and split; it does not depict temporal change. Color and marker distinguish split definitions. The control changes the learned representation and potentially the query set, so the score gap is not an acquisition-only effect.}\n\\label{fig:control}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThis is a dependence check, not proof that the low-rank model is true. The control perturbs factor learning, selection, and prediction jointly, and could also alter which targets remain. It cannot identify the best latent rank, verify that missingness is ignorable, or certify absence of benchmark contamination. All required final cases completed, and the reset replay passed; these facts support execution consistency within the tested interface, not comprehensive scientific validity.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reset_replay_ok]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the comparison identifies.}\nThe evidence is strongest for a policy-level statement: for these public score matrices, splits, and checkpoint weights, variance-guided factor reconstruction improves the observed remaining-target objective over its random-query counterpart. This is a useful decision problem when the evaluator cares precisely about whichever available benchmarks were not purchased. It is a weaker basis for a statement about reconstructing a prespecified panel, because the policy controls the scored target composition as well as the information collected. A fixed untouched panel, shared query sets for predictor ablations, and a selector-only comparison on that panel would separate these effects; none is reported here.\n\n\\paragraph{What the mechanism does not establish.}\nThe acquisition formula uses a conditional quadratic proxy with a unit-noise working interpretation. No empirical calibration of that uncertainty is available. The optimized proxy includes a coordinate that subsequently leaves the target set, and the final score uses absolute rather than squared loss. These mismatches do not invalidate the observed contrast, but they prevent interpreting it as validation of an optimality theorem. Likewise, availability-conditioned sequential choices should not be described as learning which question to ask from a new model's answers: the implemented choice ignores those answers.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope of the data distribution.}\nHistorical scores are selectively observed. Coverage filtering and the supplied availability mask restrict the population to models with enough measured benchmarks, potentially excluding the hardest prospective cases. Name-derived family labels can miss cross-namespace relationships and do not guarantee separation of pretraining data, fine-tuning lineages, or shared evaluation contamination. Historical-cell rank selection also does not measure sensitivity to tuning on entirely unseen families. The final splits and derangement control constrain some interpretations, but they do not justify arbitrary out-of-distribution or future-model generalization. Implementation interpretation uses the inspectable retained source; an independently verifiable byte-identical final snapshot is unavailable, as detailed in the supplement.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#run_baseline]]\n\n\\textbf{Means} predicts $\\mu$ regardless of observed values. \\textbf{Mean offset} adds the average purchased residual $|Q|^{-1}\\sum_{a\\in Q}(x_a-\\mu_a)$ to each benchmark mean. Both use random legal queries. Even the observation-invariant comparator is scored on its own purchased-set remainder, rather than on an identical untouched panel shared by all policies.\n\nThe factor state stores benchmark loadings and a small latent solve instead of a full benchmark covariance. Nevertheless, the inspected code initializes with a dense singular-value decomposition and forms dense products for acquisition. The factor dimension is small, but this implementation is not a sparse-matrix scalability demonstration. We report no measured memory advantage or speedup over ridge. All evaluated methods operate on CPU; no new language-model inference is performed.\n\n\\section{Experimental Protocol}\nFor many types of learners one can compute the statistically \"optimal\" way to select data. We review how these techniques have been used with feedforward neural networks [MacKay, 1992; Cohn, 1994]. We then show how the same principles may be used to select data for two alternative, statistically-based learning architectures: mixtures of Gaussians and locally weighted regression. While the techniques for neural networks are expensive and approximate, the techniques for mixtures of Gaussians and locally weighted regression are both efficient and accurate.This paper reviews the literature on Bayesian experimental design. A unified view of this topic is presented, based on a decision-theoretic approach. This framework casts criteria from the Bayesian literature of design as part of a single coherent approach. The decision-theoretic structure incorporates both linear and nonlinear design problems and it suggests possible new directions to the experimental design problem, motivated by the use of new utility functions. We show that, in some special cases of linear design problems, Bayesian solutions change in a sensible way when the prior distribution and the utility function are modified to allow for the specific structure of the experiment. The decision-theoretic approach also gives a mathematical justification for selecting the appropriate optimality criterion.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
